WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.378
A rocker takes a stab at Shakespearean horror,&nbsp;
while two SNL alums try to keep a straight face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:12.226
From Oscar nominees to MCU favorites, you won’t&nbsp;
believe who’s gotten a paycheck from Lifetime.

00:00:12.473 --> 00:00:18.800
In a 2020 interview with TV Insider, Sigourney&nbsp;
Weaver called her role as fundamentalist mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:25.600
turned LGBTQ+ advocate Mary Griffith in the&nbsp;
2009 Lifetime movie "Prayers for Bobby'' the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:31.040
hardest part she'd ever played from an emotional&nbsp;
standpoint. The role, based on the experiences of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:36.560
the real-life Griffith, required Weaver to take&nbsp;
an unsympathetic turn as a homophobic mother who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:42.400
tried to convert her son, played by Ryan Kelley,&nbsp;
after he came out as gay. After his suicide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.200
she rejects her bigoted views and becomes&nbsp;
a powerful voice in support of gay rights.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.720
"If I had just listened to my son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.360
when he poured his heart out to me,&nbsp;
I would not be standing here today."

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.240
Weaver earned Emmy, Golden Globe, and Screen&nbsp;
Actors Guild nominations for her performance as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:04.320
Griffith, who came to believe that her&nbsp;
church's interpretation of scripture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:09.840
was wrong and worked tirelessly until the end&nbsp;
of her life for pro-gay causes. Reversals of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.960
faith and deep moral searches are no small feats&nbsp;
to depict on screen, but Weaver's performance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:21.440
taps directly into Griffith's anguish over losing&nbsp;
her son to find both healing and renewed purpose.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.160
Depicting Griffith as a heartless figure wouldn't&nbsp;
have made her newfound beliefs and direction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.600
so moving, or been accurate to the real-life&nbsp;
Griffith. Weaver instead does the heavy lifting to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:37.690
find the humanity and humility in Griffith, which&nbsp;
lends extraordinary depth to her performance.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:42.720
Made at a point in Kristen Stewart's career when&nbsp;
she was just emerging from juvenile roles — and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:47.120
long before her vault to stardom in "The Twilight&nbsp;
Saga" and a string of critically praised adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:53.680
performances — the 2004 Lifetime drama "Speak"&nbsp;
confirms her exceptional acting talent. Stewart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:59.440
plays a high schooler who has difficulty talking&nbsp;
after being sexually assaulted at a house party.

00:01:59.440 --> 00:02:04.080
"Speak," which premiered at the Sundance&nbsp;
Film Festival in 2004 before airing on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:09.920
Lifetime and Showtime in 2005, is based on a&nbsp;
young adult novel by Laurie Halse Anderson.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:15.120
Both the source material and the film handle&nbsp;
their subject matter with great sensitivity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:20.400
and emotional depth. "Speak" gets tremendous&nbsp;
support from the uniformly excellent cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.280
which includes Elizabeth Perkins and D.B.&nbsp;
Sweeney as Stewart's parents, Steve Zahn as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:30.320
a sympathetic teacher, and Michael Angarano&nbsp;
as a fellow outcast who befriends Stewart.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:35.360
But it's Stewart's performance that anchors the&nbsp;
film and elevates it beyond typical Lifetime fare.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.800
The trials of her character are&nbsp;
a lot for any actor to carry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:42.269
but Stewart shoulders it&nbsp;
with strength and maturity.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:47.200
Jason Momoa's acting career was still in its&nbsp;
embryonic phase when he signed on to play a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:52.800
mostly shirtless love interest for Virginia&nbsp;
Madsen in the 2003 Lifetime movie "Tempted."

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:56.320
"You know what you need? To get&nbsp;
in the water. It's healing."&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.880
"I'm not the best swimmer."
"I am."

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:04.960
Momoa completed his first screen&nbsp;
assignment — a two-season run on "Baywatch:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:09.360
Hawaii" — shortly before signing on to&nbsp;
"Tempted." Despite its Hawaiian setting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.400
"Tempted" was actually filmed&nbsp;
in New South Wales, Australia.

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:17.520
Maggie Greenwald, a talented and award-winning&nbsp;
director of such well-regarded indie dramas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:21.040
as "The Ballad of Little Jo" and&nbsp;
"Songcatcher," benefits from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:25.760
natural beauty of her film's locations&nbsp;
and her cast. The film pairs Momoa with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.760 --> 00:03:29.920
Virginia Madsen as a woman who travels&nbsp;
with the ashes of her former guardian,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:36.240
a native Hawaiian. There, she meets Momoa, who&nbsp;
plays her late guardian's nephew, and sparks fly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:40.080
despite the fact that Madsen has a&nbsp;
husband waiting for her on the mainland.

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:44.960
Momoa, who is a naturally charismatic and&nbsp;
intense performer, doesn't have much to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.840
here beyond staring longingly while wearing as&nbsp;
little clothing as possible. If you're a fan of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:55.680
his work in "Aquaman," "Dune," and "See," this&nbsp;
isn't an exemplary showcase for his abilities.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:01.077
But if you're interested in his chiseled build,&nbsp;
"Tempted" is a two-hour box of eye candy.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:07.200
Though Mark Ruffalo is one of Hollywood's busiest&nbsp;
and most acclaimed actors today, the Emmy, Grammy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.520
Tony, and Oscar nominee was largely anonymous in&nbsp;
the 1990s. Ruffalo's resume during this period&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:19.600
included low-budget horror and indie films, but&nbsp;
he also kept busy with occasional forays into TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:24.960
like "On the Second Day of Christmas,"&nbsp;
which aired on Lifetime Television in 1997.

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:29.760
Directed by James Frawley, "On the Second Day&nbsp;
of Christmas" takes place in a department store&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:35.200
during the holidays, but it's no "Miracle on&nbsp;
34th Street." Despite being a little girl,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:40.560
Patsy, played by Lauren Pratt, is an experienced&nbsp;
pickpocket who works as a team with her aunt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:45.040
played by Mary Stuart Masterson. Ruffalo&nbsp;
is the department store security guard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:49.040
who catches them in the act, so&nbsp;
writer Brian Hohlfeld bends logic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:53.040
to force the trio to spend Christmas&nbsp;
together while awaiting sentencing.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:56.880
"Christmas is supposed to be a time of compassion,&nbsp;
don't you feel the least bit sorry for us?"&nbsp;

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.840
"Look lady, you choose a life of crime,&nbsp;
you gotta take the consequences."

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:03.520
While many holiday romance couples&nbsp;
seem forced together by the plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.520 --> 00:05:08.839
Ruffalo and Masterson's chemistry helps to&nbsp;
make their eventual connection seem believable.

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.400
In 2011, Lifetime presented "Five," an&nbsp;
anthology feature featuring stories about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:20.400
the impact of breast cancer on patients and their&nbsp;
families. The project, created by Marta Kauffman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:25.760
drew high-profile talent behind the camera,&nbsp;
including Jennifer Aniston, Alicia Keyes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:31.680
and Patty Jenkins. It also attracted talented&nbsp;
performers like Patricia Clarkson, Tracee Ellis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:37.440
Ross, Rosario Dawson, and Tony Shalhoub. Two&nbsp;
years later, Lifetime returned to the "Five"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:42.480
format for "Call Me Crazy," which focused its&nbsp;
quintet of stories on mental health issues.

00:05:42.480 --> 00:05:46.720
"Five" gained an Emmy Award nomination for&nbsp;
casting and numerous nominations for its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:53.040
actors and directors, so when the network decided&nbsp;
to return to the format for 2013's "Call Me Crazy:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:58.240
A Five Film," they garnered an even more&nbsp;
impressive roster of talent. Directors included&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:04.480
Oscar winner Laura Dern, Bryce Dallas Howard,&nbsp;
Ashley Judd, and Bonnie Hunt, while three Oscar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:11.023
winners – Jennifer Hudson, Octavia Spencer, and&nbsp;
Melissa Leo – each headlined one of the stories.

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.160
By the age of 20, Kirsten Dunst had amassed&nbsp;
an impressive body of work in films and on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.960
television: She earned her first of four&nbsp;
Golden Globe nominations at the age of 10&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:26.000
for "Interview with the Vampire," and featured in&nbsp;
such popular and critical hits as "Little Women,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:31.760
"Jumanji," "Wag the Dog," and "The Virgin&nbsp;
Suicides." During this incredibly busy period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:38.080
the future Oscar and Emmy nominee also starred in&nbsp;
a Lifetime original movie, "Fifteen and Pregnant."

00:06:38.080 --> 00:06:43.680
Dunst is the titular teenager, Tina, who becomes&nbsp;
pregnant by her deadbeat boyfriend Ray, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:49.760
by Daniel Kountz. She decides to keep the baby,&nbsp;
causing friction between her separated parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:53.360
played by Park Overall and&nbsp;
David Andrews, and her sister,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:57.360
played by Julia Whelan. The film&nbsp;
hits all the pregnancy drama tropes:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:03.200
Tina's dramatic reveal, Ray bailing on being a&nbsp;
dad, and a birth sequence filled with screaming.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:08.160
But it also does well in dividing the focus&nbsp;
between Tina and her extended family and friends.

00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:10.480
"You're not my leader, I&nbsp;
don't act and think like you."

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:12.560
"Well it wouldn't be so terrible&nbsp;
for you to be a little less shallow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:13.760
maybe a little more responsible."

00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.520
"You're not that great either, you know."

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:20.240
Dunst portrays the confusion of teen life&nbsp;
and the upheaval of an unexpected pregnancy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:24.480
with the same depth and confidence she's&nbsp;
shown throughout her career. The majority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.480 --> 00:07:28.560
of her scenes pit her against Overall&nbsp;
and Andrews — two extremely capable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.560 --> 00:07:32.977
stage-trained performers — and&nbsp;
she holds her own in every scene.

00:07:33.440 --> 00:07:39.200
The drama "Stockholm, Pennsylvania" surprised many&nbsp;
Sundance filmgoers by premiering on Lifetime in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:45.760
2015 rather than hitting theaters after garnering&nbsp;
mostly positive reviews at the festival that year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:51.600
The film features an impressive cast led by Oscar&nbsp;
nominee Saoirse Ronan and Emmy winner Cynthia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:58.160
Nixon and tackles a difficult subject: the impact&nbsp;
of an abduction on both the victim and her family.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:04.720
Ronan plays 22-year-old Leia, who was held captive&nbsp;
from the age of four by a man with apocalyptic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:10.800
delusions named Ben, played by Jason Isaacs.&nbsp;
Eventually, she returns to her parents, played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:16.320
by Nixon and David Warshofsky, that she barely&nbsp;
knows. What follows is, at times, an even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.520
intense take on life after trauma than is depicted&nbsp;
in "Room" with Brie Larson: Leia's refusal to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:27.360
accept her parents and fond recollections of life&nbsp;
with Ben. All signs point to Stockholm Syndrome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:32.480
Ronan avoids the pitfall of overplaying a&nbsp;
deeply damaged character: She remains quiet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:37.807
instead of explosive or unhinged, allowing the&nbsp;
viewer to guess at her true emotional state.

00:08:38.240 --> 00:08:41.920
When Lifetime steps out of the romantic&nbsp;
thriller or suburban drama box,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:46.880
the end result is often a high-profile project&nbsp;
like "Call Me Crazy" or "Betty and Coretta."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.240
Trying something different rarely meant casting&nbsp;
Courtney Love, Dominic Monaghan, and Paul Walter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:56.800
Hauser in a horror-themed series based on&nbsp;
Shakespeare's "A Midsummer's Night Dream."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.800 --> 00:09:02.240
Of course, that's exactly what Lifetime&nbsp;
provided with 2017's "A Midsummer's Nightmare."

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.920
Produced in partnership with A&amp;E Studios after&nbsp;
the success of their hit drama "UnREAL," Lifetime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:12.160
appeared to be aiming for "American Horror&nbsp;
Story" territory with "A Midsummer's Nightmare."

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:16.240
"A Midsummer's Nightmare" opens on a strong&nbsp;
note, with a character dragged through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:21.840
woods by masked figures that hurl her into an&nbsp;
open grave filled with bees. Once that initial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:27.265
shock fades away, however, the film becomes a&nbsp;
routine mix of trysting and low-wattage mystery.

00:09:27.265 --> 00:09:30.448
"Okay, campers? Time to scamper."

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:36.080
"A Deadly Adoption" is sort of a meta-Lifetime&nbsp;
movie. It's a parody of the network's sudsy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.080 --> 00:09:40.320
plotting with Will Ferrell and Kristin Wiig&nbsp;
playing the sort of hapless suburbanites&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:45.360
usually portrayed by the likes of Eric Roberts and&nbsp;
Kellie Martin. Though produced by Ferrell's former&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.000
partner Adam McKay, "A Deadly Adoption"&nbsp;
unfolds with an entirely straight face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.880
requiring the audience to find the laughs in&nbsp;
the inherent absurdity of the story itself.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:58.400
Ferrell and Wiig, who work hard to&nbsp;
achieve a sort of earnest blandness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:02.400
are Robert and Sarah, a married couple&nbsp;
who lose their second child in an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.400 --> 00:10:07.120
unfortunate dock accident. They soon decide&nbsp;
on using a surrogate to have another child,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.120 --> 00:10:11.840
but their prospective birth mother Bridgette,&nbsp;
played by Lifetime vet Jessica Lowndes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:17.760
is more than she seems: she's actually obsessed&nbsp;
with Robert. Ferrell's trademark near-hysteria and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:23.440
Wiig's overdramatic reveries are put to good use&nbsp;
here, but the humor is almost too subtle: there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:29.021
just a razor-thin margin between the comedy&nbsp;
and the histrionics of a real Lifetime movie.

